                <p class="snippet" >
                    In <b>Moge v. Moge</b>, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 813, at p. 848, L’Heureux‐Dubé J., writing on behalf of the majority of the Court, stated that “the support provisions of the Act are intended to deal with the economic consequences, for both parties, of the marriage or its breakdown” (emphasis in original). &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;This Court specifically rejected such a theory in <b>Moge v. Moge</b>, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 813, at p. 874, and I can see no reason why it should be exhumed here. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In <b>Moge v. Moge</b>, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 813, at pp. 853-54 and 861-62, this Court made numerous observations on the feminization of poverty and disproportionate impact of divorce on women and children. 
