                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Referred to: Andrews v. Law Society of British Columbia, [1989] 1 S.C.R. 143; R. v. Lyons, [1987] 2 S.C.R. 309; R. v. Tran, [1994] 2 S.C.R. 951; <b>Moge v. Moge</b>, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 813; Santosky v. Kramer, 455 U.S. 745 (1982); B. (R.) v. Children’s Aid Society of Metropolitan Toronto, [1995] 1 S.C.R. 315; R. v. Morgentaler, &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;As I wrote in <b>Moge v. Moge</b>, [1992] 3 S.C.R. 813, at p. 853, “In Canada, the feminization of poverty is an entrenched social phenomenon.” The patterns of relationships within marriage disproportionately lead to women taking responsibility for child care, foregoing economic opportunities in the workforce, and suffering 
                </p>
